      "explanation": "The qpdf command-line examples (lines 270-340) demonstrate subprocess/shell execution patterns without warning about command injection risks. Examples like `qpdf --split-pages=3 input.pdf output_group_%02d.pdf` could be exploited if user-supplied filenames or parameters are not properly escaped. An attacker could pass crafted filenames containing shell metacharacters (e.g., `input; rm -rf /;.pdf`) to execute arbitrary commands when these are passed to bash without proper quoting.",
      "remediation": "Add prominent security warnings about: (1) Command injection prevention - always use subprocess module with `shell=False` and pass arguments as list, never string concatenation; (2) Input sanitization - validate and escape filenames before passing to qpdf; (3) Use safer alternatives - Python libraries like pypdf/pypdfium2 instead of shell commands where possible. Specific example: Use `subprocess.run(['qpdf', '--split-pages=3', input_file, output_template], shell=False)` instead of shell string execution. Include explicit code showing the WRONG way (vulnerable) vs. RIGHT way (safe).",
